        <h3>Why Generator Sizing Exceeds Motor Requirements</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Starting surge:</strong> Electric motors require 3-5x running current during startup</li>
          <li><strong>Multiple motors:</strong> Conveyors, water pumps, and accessories add to total load</li>
          <li><strong>Power factor:</strong> AC motors typically run at 0.8-0.9 power factor</li>
          <li><strong>Altitude derating:</strong> Generators lose capacity at elevation</li>
          <li><strong>Temperature derating:</strong> Hot weather reduces generator output</li>
          <li><strong>Safety margin:</strong> Avoid overloading generator for reliability</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="highlight-stat">
          <span class="number">25-50%</span>
          <span class="label">Generator oversizing recommended for reliable operation</span>
        </div>

        <h2>Diesel Generator Options</h2>
        <p>Most mining operations use diesel generators due to remote locations and fuel availability worldwide.</p>

        <h3>Generator Types and Applications:</h3>

        <div class="grid-3">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Portable Generators</h3>
            <p><strong>Size:</strong> 75-200 HP<br>
            <strong>Best for:</strong> Small wash plants, temporary setups<br>
            <strong>Advantages:</strong> Lower cost, easy transport<br>
            <strong>Disadvantages:</strong> Higher fuel consumption, less reliable</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Skid-Mounted Units</h3>
            <p><strong>Size:</strong> 150-500 HP<br>
            <strong>Best for:</strong> Medium to large operations<br>
            <strong>Advantages:</strong> Better efficiency, more reliable<br>
            <strong>Disadvantages:</strong> Requires crane for positioning</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Containerized Generators</h3>
            <p><strong>Size:</strong> 300+ HP<br>
            <strong>Best for:</strong> Large permanent operations<br>
            <strong>Advantages:</strong> Weather protection, sound attenuation<br>
            <strong>Disadvantages:</strong> Higher initial cost</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3>Fuel Consumption Factors:</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Load factor:</strong> Generators are most efficient at 70-80% load</li>
          <li><strong>Engine condition:</strong> Well-maintained engines use 15-20% less fuel</li>
          <li><strong>Altitude:</strong> Higher elevations increase fuel consumption</li>
          <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> Hot weather reduces efficiency</li>
          <li><strong>Fuel quality:</strong> Clean fuel improves performance and longevity</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Electric Hookup Option</h2>
        <p>Electric power from the grid is rare in mining but offers advantages when available.</p>

        <h3>When Electric Power Makes Sense:</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Quarry operations:</strong> Established sites with existing electrical infrastructure</li>
          <li><strong>Urban mining:</strong> Construction sand and gravel operations near cities</li>
          <li><strong>Seasonal operations:</strong> Short-term utility hookups may be economical</li>
          <li><strong>Permanent installations:</strong> Long-term operations justifying electrical installation</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Electric Power Advantages:</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Lower operating costs:</strong> Electricity typically costs 50-70% less than diesel</li>
          <li><strong>No fuel logistics:</strong> Eliminates fuel delivery and storage</li>
          <li><strong>Consistent power:</strong> No voltage fluctuations or power derating</li>
          <li><strong>Environmental benefits:</strong> Lower emissions and noise</li>
          <li><strong>Less maintenance:</strong> No generator maintenance requirements</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Electric Power Disadvantages:</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Infrastructure costs:</strong> $10,000-50,000+ for utility hookup</li>
          <li><strong>Limited availability:</strong> Most mining locations lack grid access</li>
          <li><strong>Utility reliability:</strong> Power outages stop operations</li>
          <li><strong>Demand charges:</strong> Commercial rates include peak demand penalties</li>
        </ul>

