        <h2>Generator Placement and Installation</h2>
        <p>Proper generator placement is crucial for safety, efficiency, and maintenance access.</p>

        <h3>Placement Guidelines:</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Distance from wash plant:</strong> 50-100 feet minimum to reduce noise and vibration</li>
          <li><strong>Level surface:</strong> Generators must be level for proper oil circulation</li>
          <li><strong>Ventilation:</strong> Adequate airflow for cooling and exhaust dispersion</li>
          <li><strong>Access roads:</strong> Fuel truck and service vehicle access</li>
          <li><strong>Drainage:</strong> Prevent standing water around generator</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Weather Protection:</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Shelter options:</strong> Temporary canopies, permanent buildings, or containerized units</li>
          <li><strong>Rain protection:</strong> Prevent water ingress into electrical components</li>
          <li><strong>Wind protection:</strong> Reduce cooling air disruption in high winds</li>
          <li><strong>Sun protection:</strong> Reduce overheating in hot climates</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Electrical Connections:</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Cable sizing:</strong> Use appropriate gauge wire for distance and amperage</li>
          <li><strong>Voltage drop:</strong> Keep cable runs short to minimize voltage loss</li>
          <li><strong>Safety disconnects:</strong> Install lockout/tagout switches</li>
          <li><strong>Grounding:</strong> Proper grounding prevents electrical hazards</li>
        </ul>

