        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        
        <h3>Can I use a smaller generator to save on fuel costs?</h3>
        <p>Undersized generators cause voltage drops, motor overheating, and premature failure. The extra fuel cost of proper sizing is far less than repair and replacement costs from running underpowered equipment.</p>

        <h3>How does altitude affect my generator requirements?</h3>
        <p>Generators lose approximately 3-4% of their power output per 1,000 feet of elevation. At 5,000 feet elevation, you need 15-20% larger generator to maintain the same power output.</p>

        <h3>What about power factor and electrical load calculations?</h3>
        <p>Electric motors typically run at 0.8-0.9 power factor. This means a 100 HP motor (75 kW) actually requires about 85-95 kVA from the generator. Always account for power factor when sizing generators.</p>

        <h3>Should I run the generator at full load for fuel efficiency?</h3>
        <p>Generators are most fuel-efficient at 70-80% load. Running consistently above 90% load reduces engine life, while running below 50% load causes carbon buildup and poor fuel economy.</p>

        <h3>How often should I change generator oil in dusty mining conditions?</h3>
        <p>Dusty conditions require more frequent oil changes—typically every 100-150 hours instead of standard 250-hour intervals. Monitor oil condition and change when it becomes contaminated or breaks down.</p>
